WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:10.107
Take a phone photo of a room with a bright window, and the window often turns into a flat white blob. The frame, the trees outside, all gone.

2
00:00:10.457 --> 00:00:17.518
Our eyes handle it fine. The screen cannot. A screen can only show colours from black up to a fixed white.

3
00:00:17.868 --> 00:00:25.719
Real light has no such ceiling. In this loft, the brightest spot is about forty eight times brighter than the screen's white.

4
00:00:26.069 --> 00:00:30.378
So how do you squeeze all that light onto a screen, without losing the window?

5
00:00:31.578 --> 00:00:39.877
A renderer first computes real amounts of light. This is called scene referred. Here is the same loft, coloured by brightness.

6
00:00:40.227 --> 00:00:50.253
Green and blue are ordinary surfaces. Red means more than one, brighter than any screen white. The window glass and its reflection in the brass sphere.

7
00:00:50.603 --> 00:00:56.214
Those values are not errors. They are true light. The question is only how to display them.

8
00:00:57.414 --> 00:01:01.489
The simplest answer is to clip. Anything above one becomes one.

9
00:01:01.839 --> 00:01:10.948
In our frame, about thirteen point four percent of pixels have at least one colour channel above one. Clipping burns all of them to flat white.

10
00:01:11.298 --> 00:01:16.994
Look at the window reflected in the brass. Clipped, the window bars are gone. A white pancake.

11
00:01:18.194 --> 00:01:23.549
Tone mapping does something smarter. Instead of a hard wall at one, it uses a curve.

12
00:01:23.899 --> 00:01:26.715
Here is clipping. A straight line, then a wall.

13
00:01:27.065 --> 00:01:35.555
Here is Khronos P B R Neutral. Through the darks and middle tones it stays almost one to one, so colours look as designed.

14
00:01:35.905 --> 00:01:42.263
Then it bends into a soft shoulder. Bright values are squeezed closer and closer to white, but never cut.

15
00:01:42.613 --> 00:01:53.023
And here is the aha. Clipping throws the brights away. A tone curve folds them in. With the neutral curve, no pixel is clipped at all, and the window bars come back.

16
00:01:54.223 --> 00:01:59.194
This is the clipping mask. Red marks every pixel that clipping would burn to white.

17
00:01:59.544 --> 00:02:04.024
The neutral curve keeps all of these, compressed, with their structure intact.

18
00:02:05.224 --> 00:02:12.179
Different curves make different choices. Look at the average of the middle tones, which starts at zero point four eight.

19
00:02:12.529 --> 00:02:21.659
Reinhard's classic curve, light over one plus light, pulls them down to zero point three two five. The glaze and concrete look dimmer.

20
00:02:22.009 --> 00:02:29.839
The popular A C E S film curve pushes them up to zero point six zero four. Punchy, film like contrast.

21
00:02:30.189 --> 00:02:38.487
The neutral curve lands at zero point four four one. Closest to the original. That is its goal, to show products as they are.

22
00:02:39.687 --> 00:02:48.221
Curves can also change colour. Apply a curve to red, green and blue separately, and a bright brass highlight drifts toward grey.

23
00:02:48.571 --> 00:02:59.387
Every per channel curve is secretly a colour change. The neutral curve instead compresses brightness, then desaturates toward white on purpose, in a controlled way.

24
00:03:00.587 --> 00:03:06.347
One more knob. Exposure is a simple gain before the curve, like a camera's exposure.

25
00:03:06.697 --> 00:03:21.865
At exposure zero point five, the middle tones average zero point one eight nine. At one point zero five, zero point four four one. At two, zero point eight one seven. And nothing clips at any of them.

26
00:03:22.215 --> 00:03:30.364
Notice what the curve never does. It does not create light. It only decides which screen value each amount of light receives.

27
00:03:31.564 --> 00:03:40.588
What this does not prove. This is one loft scene, tone mapped in software for a standard screen. Not H D R television output.

28
00:03:40.938 --> 00:03:47.786
A C E S is not wrong. It aims for film contrast. Reinhard and clipping are here as controls.

29
00:03:48.136 --> 00:03:56.413
A single error number cannot rank highlight detail, so we judged structure with the mask and the zoom. All thirty one checks passed.

30
00:03:57.613 --> 00:04:04.952
The takeaway. Render real light first. Then set the exposure. Then fold it onto the screen with a named curve.

31
00:04:05.302 --> 00:04:08.502
Clipping cuts the brights. A tone curve bends them.

32
00:04:08.852 --> 00:04:17.492
Film and game pipelines lean on curves like A C E S. Khronos published P B R Neutral for showing products true to colour.

33
00:04:17.842 --> 00:04:29.063
For further reading, see Reinhard and colleagues on photographic tone reproduction, Narkowicz's A C E S fit, and the Khronos P B R Neutral specification.
